<article dtd-version="1.3" article-type="meeting-abstract" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="hwp">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">ASCOMTG</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Clinical Oncology</journal-title><abbrev-journal-title>ASCO MEETING ABSTRACTS</abbrev-journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0732-183X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1527-7755</issn><publisher><publisher-name>American Society of Clinical Oncology</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="meeting-id">335</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-id">17304</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-participation-id">8017800</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="temporary-abstract-id">31849</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="abstract-id">rw31849</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1177/1533033820957000</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Biology - Cancer</subject></subj-group><series-title>2026 ASCO Annual Meeting (May 29 - June 2, 2026)</series-title><series-text>Publication Only</series-text></article-categories><title-group><article-title>MicroRNA-663 Regulates Melanoma Progression by Inhibiting FHL3</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib id="C8017800" contrib-type="presenter"><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>Saijun</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8017800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8017801" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hu</surname><given-names>Yunfeng</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8017800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8017802" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wu</surname><given-names>Shi</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8017800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8017803" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>He</surname><given-names>Yong</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8017800" /></contrib><contrib id="C8017804" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Deng</surname><given-names>Liehua</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8017800" /></contrib><aff id="A8017800">Department of Dermatology, The First Affiliated Hospital of Jinan University, Guangzhou, China;</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2020</year></pub-date><fpage>rw31849</fpage><lpage>rw31849</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>ASCO-style conversion for internal testing; source rights remain with the original publisher.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2020</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p><bold>rw31849</bold></p><p>microRNA-663a (miR-663a) was reported to be highly expressed in cancers. However, its roles in melanoma progression remain unclear. Reverse transcription quantitative polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR) was conducted to measure miR-663a expression level in melanoma cell lines and normal cells. Cell counting kit-8 assay, wound-healing assay, and transwell invasion assay were conducted to analyze biological roles of miR-663a in melanoma. Luciferase activity reporter assay was conducted to validate the connection of miR-663a and Four and a half LIM domain (FHL) protein 3 (FHL3) in melanoma. Our results showed miR-663a expression level was significantly increased in melanoma cells compared with normal cells. Silencing miR-663a expression suppresses melanoma cell proliferation, migration, and invasion</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-row-number</meta-name><meta-value>178</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-record-id</meta-name><meta-value>31849.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-journal</meta-name><meta-value>Technology in Cancer Research &amp; Treatment</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-publisher</meta-name><meta-value>SAGE Publications</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-country</meta-name><meta-value>China</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-article-type</meta-name><meta-value>Research Article;</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>manual-verdict</meta-name><meta-value>YES</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-reason</meta-name><meta-value>Investigation by Journal/Publisher;Paper Mill;Unreliable Results and/or Conclusions;</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front></article>
